# ClassBreak mobile environment variables
#
# Copy this file to mobile/.env.local, set EXPO_PUBLIC_API_URL, then restart the
# Expo dev server with `npx expo start --clear` (env vars are inlined at bundle
# time, so a plain reload does not pick up changes).
#
# There is deliberately no default and no sample IP here: a real-looking address
# that does not exist on your network fails with a confusing network error
# instead of an obvious configuration problem.

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# EXPO_PUBLIC_API_URL — base URL of the ClassBreak backend, including scheme,
# host, port, and no trailing slash.
#
# REQUIRED for native builds. Leave the right-hand side empty until you have
# your own value; the app will show a setup message rather than a network error.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
EXPO_PUBLIC_API_URL=

# How to fill in the value for each target:
#
# 1) Physical Android phone on the same Wi-Fi as your computer
#    a. Find this computer's LAN IP:
#         Linux:   hostname -I | awk '{print $1}'        (or: ip -4 addr show scope global)
#         macOS:   ipconfig getifaddr en0
#         Windows: ipconfig   -> "IPv4 Address" under your active adapter
#       Use a private-range address (192.168.x.x, 10.x.x.x, 172.16-31.x.x).
#    b. Start the backend on all interfaces (loopback-only is unreachable):
#         python -m uvicorn app.main:app --host 0.0.0.0 --port 8000
#    c. Set the value, substituting YOUR address:
#         EXPO_PUBLIC_API_URL=http://<your-computer-LAN-IP>:8000
#    d. Check it from the phone's browser first:
#         http://<your-computer-LAN-IP>:8000/api/health
#       If that fails, the app cannot connect either: fix Wi-Fi isolation,
#       firewall (allow TCP 8000), or the bind address.
#
# 2) Android emulator (the emulator reaches the host at the special 10.0.2.2):
#      EXPO_PUBLIC_API_URL=http://10.0.2.2:8000
#
# 3) USB-connected device with port forwarding (no LAN IP needed):
#      adb reverse tcp:8000 tcp:8000
#      EXPO_PUBLIC_API_URL=http://localhost:8000
#
# 4) Web build served by Expo (`npx expo start --web`):
#      EXPO_PUBLIC_API_URL=http://localhost:8000
#    (When unset, the web build alone falls back to http://localhost:8000.)
#
# IMPORTANT: EXPO_PUBLIC_* values are compiled into the app bundle and are
# public. Only backend URLs belong here — never API keys or other secrets.
